     "type": "example",
     "title": "A Baseball vs. an Electron",
     "content": "Let's put real numbers into **λ = h / p** and see why you never watch a baseball diffract.\n\n**The baseball.** Mass ≈ 0.145 kg, thrown at 40 m/s. Its momentum is p = mv = 0.145 × 40 ≈ **5.8 kg·m/s**. So:\n\nλ = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴) / 5.8 ≈ **1.1 × 10⁻³⁴ meters.**\n\nThat wavelength is a hundred-trillion-trillion times smaller than an atomic nucleus. No slit that tiny exists, so the baseball's waviness is utterly undetectable. It just acts like a solid object.\n\n**The electron.** Mass ≈ 9.1 × 10⁻³¹ kg. Give it a modest speed of about 7 × 10⁶ m/s (typical after a small voltage). Then p ≈ 6.4 × 10⁻²⁴ kg·m/s, and:\n\nλ = (6.6 × 10⁻³⁴) / (6.4 × 10⁻²⁴) ≈ **1 × 10⁻¹⁰ meters.**\n\nThat's about the width of an atom — and crucially, comparable to gaps we can actually manufacture. So the electron's wave nature shows up loud and clear. Same equation, wildly different worlds, all because of that heavy p in the denominator.",
